From 70af6a038109804fc51fc9607737d84d33e9185d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (for Kush)" <kushisthecoolest@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:23:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] Linux interop: runtime FD contract, binary semaphores;
 DLSS opt-in

GlInterop on Linux now follows the runtime's dxvk_GetInteropInfo
contract:
- Every handle field of every call is a fresh, caller-owned file
  descriptor. Each one is imported, which hands it to GL, or closed.
  The semaphores are imported once and their later descriptors closed.
  The image is re-imported only when its size, format or allocation
  size changes, never because the descriptor number changed.
- The semaphores are binary. They are plain glGenSemaphoresEXT objects
  instead of NV timeline semaphores; glCreateSemaphoresNV returned 0 in
  game and failed init. No timeline values are set.
- GL_TEXTURE_TILING_EXT = GL_OPTIMAL_TILING_EXT is set before the
  memory is bound.
- dxvkDone is waited exactly once per Remix Present, and only after an
  image was imported, so never before the first Present (GetInteropInfo
  has no image then). A signal no frame consumed is consumed before the
  next Present.
- clientDone is never signalled: the runtime never waits on it.
  glFinish before Present orders GL's reads of the image before the
  runtime's next blit into it. The GL->interop copy is skipped: the
  runtime overwrites the image at Present and GLFW presents.
The Linux rules live in InteropHandles; Windows behaviour is unchanged.

stageLinuxRuntime stages the DLSS libraries only with -PlinuxDlss=true
or -PlinuxDlssDir, and removes ones left from an earlier run, so the
JAR no longer ships them by default.

InteropHandlesTest covers the image-identity and wait decisions.

